  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening Access to Neonatal Care among the Displaced Population in Borno State, Nigeria</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through partnership and collaboration across the private section and humanitarian actors in Borno state, the project will build on existing health service provision in the internally displaced persons (IDP) communities. Currently, there are pediatric stabilization centers in 3 IDP local government area and safe spaces for obstetric care in IDP camps and camp-like settings. The stabilization center is a government health facility supported with technical pediatric operations by IRC. While the center serves as a referral center for general pediatric cases from surrounding IDP communities, there is no dedicated space for neonatal care. Also, there are safe spaces for obstetric care typically in PHC facilities in IDP camps. These safe spaces are technically operated with support from UNFPA and provide antenatal, delivery and post-natal care for IDP. Key interventions will include 1) the creation of neonatal space- &#x2018;neo-rooms&#x2019; in the pediatric centers 2) design and testing of a tricycle neonatal ambulance that utilizes dual energy system 3) capacity building on neonatal care using the provided technologies. Humanitarian private sector partnership- the project implementation will include identification of private manufacturing company that will partner with IOM in the design of the neonatal devices and ambulatory system using locally available alternative energy solution.</narrative>
  </description>
